  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nA stretch of pleasant weather is on deck for the next couple of days. \nHigh temperatures will gradually warm from the upper 50s/lower 60s \nto the mid-upper 60s by mid-week. The culprit is tied to increasing \nheights aloft and the building influence of a surface high pressure \nthrough the first half of the work week. This will keep us on the \neastern edge, but do enough to keep the weather generally mild & \ndry. The ridge in question will build over the central CONUS. \n\nThis will allow split flow to develop with a shortwave ejecting out \nacross the southern tier Tuesday. This trough will slowly track \neastward and should lead to an increase in sky cover on Tuesday. By \nWednesday, our northern stream provides us with a deep trough \nprogged to drop south out of Canada into the Great Lakes region.\nUpstream, the mid level ridge begins to build north into central \nCanada as the pattern becomes more amplified. The northern stream \njet will be forced south into the SE CONUS by Thursday in response \nto the building ridge upstream. Ultimately, we end up with an Omega \nblock-like pattern by late week as another trough digs in along the \nCalifornia coast. Much of the eastern CONUS will see a shift toward \na cooler, damper pattern as several lobes of vorticity move through \nthe cyclonic flow of the Great Lakes low. Each disturbance will bring \nmultiple rounds of reinforcing cold air from Canada into the \nsoutheast US. Colder air will settle in by the end of the work week \nwith high temperatures only in the upper 40s to lower 50s by Friday. \nColder air will not be the only concern as precipitation chances \nbegin to increase Wednesday night into Thursday. Each weak \ndisturbance that pivots around the parent Great Lakes low will bring \nan increased chance of precipitation through Friday. \n\nThere is increasing confidence given the run to run consistency \nacross various models and ensemble guidance. We will need to monitor \nthe potential for the first accumulating snowfall of the season at \nsome spots in the mountains. The combination of northwest flow and \ncold air aloft would be enough to support snow chances at the higher \nelevations of the southern Appalachians.",
